# What goes to a connection.
#
# Either bytes that are already what should be written, or a message the
# connection has to say in its own protocol.
#
# The queue rendering a message was fine while there was one protocol. There
# are two now — RillMQ's own and AMQP — and a delivery in AMQP carries a tag
# the queue could not know, because the tag counts deliveries on that
# connection rather than messages in that queue. So the queue says what
# happened and the connection says it.
type Out
  OFrame(text: Str)
  # `props` is whatever a client put in the message's header, kept exactly as
  # it sent it. RillMQ has no use for any of it and hands it back byte for
  # byte, which is the only way `reply-to` and `correlation-id` can mean on the
  # way out what they meant on the way in.
  ODeliver(queue: Str, id: Int, body: Str, props: Str)
  # A message that is now on the disk. RillMQ's own protocol says `+OK <id>`;
  # AMQP says `Basic.Ack` and only when the client asked for confirms. Both
  # are the same event, so the event is what travels.
  OPublished(queue: Str, id: Int)
